-- M2: statutory citations on the verdict screen, and a glossary of L4 defined
-- terms.
--
-- Three sub-decisions, each carrying a statutory `@ref`, conjoined by AND so
-- that a FALSE first conjunct short-circuits the other two away.  The
-- docassemble backend must:
--
--   * attach each rule's citation to the `code:` block that decides it, via
--     docassemble's `explain()` (util.py:13227), so that the verdict screen's
--     `logic_explanation()` (util.py:13259) names EXACTLY the rules that
--     actually fired, in evaluation order, and omits the short-circuited ones;
--   * strip L4's own `@ref ` herald and the inline `<< >>` delimiters, so a
--     citation reads as prose and never as L4 syntax;
--   * escape Mako-hostile citation text — the third citation deliberately
--     BEGINS with `%` (a Mako control line at start of line) and contains a
--     literal `${ ... }` (a Mako expression), the same hostility the
--     `defaults` example already applies to `@desc` (R9.1);
--   * emit an `auto terms:` glossary block whose entries are the L4 defined
--     terms — the `DECLARE`d type and each `MEANS`-bound sub-decision —
--     defined by their `@desc` text.
--
-- The two #EVALs below are the round-trip oracle AND the citation oracle:
--   * the "exempt" case fires all three rules, so all three citations appear,
--     in declaration order;
--   * the "cap exceeded" case fires only the first, so ONLY the first
--     citation may appear — the other two rules were never reached, and a
--     verdict screen that cites them would be citing law that did not decide
--     anything.

@desc A securities offering made in reliance on the crowdfunding exemption
DECLARE Offering
    HAS
      @desc The aggregate amount sold in reliance on the exemption in the preceding 12 months, in dollars
      `amount raised in 12 months`    IS A NUMBER
      @desc Whether the whole offering was conducted through a single intermediary
      `sold through one intermediary` IS A BOOLEAN
      @desc Whether that intermediary is registered with the Commission as a funding portal
      `intermediary is registered`    IS A BOOLEAN

@export Whether this offering qualifies for the crowdfunding exemption
GIVEN o IS AN Offering @desc The offering under assessment
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `offering exempt` o IF
        `within the annual cap`
    AND `sold through a single intermediary`
    AND `the intermediary is registered`
 WHERE
    @ref 17 CFR 227.100(a)(1) — offering maximum
    @desc The amount sold in reliance on the exemption in the preceding 12 months does not exceed $5,000,000
    `within the annual cap` MEANS
        o's `amount raised in 12 months` AT MOST 5000000

    <<17 CFR 227.100(a)(3) — sales through one intermediary only>>
    @desc The offering is conducted exclusively through a single intermediary
    `sold through a single intermediary` MEANS
        o's `sold through one intermediary`

    @ref % of the proceeds retained is set out at ${ fee_schedule } — 17 CFR 227.300(a)
    @desc The intermediary is registered with the Commission as a funding portal
    `the intermediary is registered` MEANS
        o's `intermediary is registered`

-- Exempt: all three rules fire, so the verdict screen must cite all three,
-- in declaration order.
#EVAL `offering exempt` OF
        Offering
        WITH
            `amount raised in 12 months`    IS 1000000
            `sold through one intermediary` IS TRUE
            `intermediary is registered`    IS TRUE
-- TRUE

-- Cap exceeded: the first conjunct is FALSE, so the second and third rules
-- are short-circuited away and never decide anything. The verdict screen must
-- cite 17 CFR 227.100(a)(1) and NOTHING ELSE.
#EVAL `offering exempt` OF
        Offering
        WITH
            `amount raised in 12 months`    IS 9000000
            `sold through one intermediary` IS TRUE
            `intermediary is registered`    IS TRUE
-- FALSE
